<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Waste Land (T. S. Eliot)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1922</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted at the climax of the poem</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">My Fair Lady</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1956</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the title of the musical may come from its last line</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">My Whole World Is Falling Down (Brenda Lee)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1963</span><span class="sg-chain-h">chorus loosely based on it</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Walls of Jericho (Helloween)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1985</span><span class="sg-chain-h">music of the first track based on it</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Knippelsbro Går Op og Ned</span><span class="sg-chain-h">similar Danish bridge rhyme, perhaps the source</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Die Magdeburger Brück</span><span class="sg-chain-h">similar German bridge rhyme</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pont chus</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">16th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">similar French bridge rhyme</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Le porte</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">14th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">similar Italian bridge rhyme</span></li></ul></section>
